<div class="card" style="margin-top:1rem"><h3>B. Oscilloscope</h3><canvas id="sc" height="440"></canvas>
<div class="btns"><button id="pz">Pause</button><button id="rs">Reset simulation</button></div>
<p class="mu">Rows: carrier, AM signal, detector output, final audio. Conceptual model; carrier drawn at a greatly reduced frequency.</p><p class="mu det" style="font-size:.9rem">Rows: (1) RF carrier, (2) AM carrier, (3) detector output with recovered envelope, (4) final audio. The carrier is drawn at a vastly reduced frequency so it is visible; real AM carriers are 530–1700 kHz while audio is ≈ 20 Hz–5 kHz. Model assumption: half-wave detector with asymmetry k, followed by ideal low-pass filtering (mean over a carrier cycle ∝ k).</p></div>
<div class="card" style="margin-top:1rem"><h3>E. Audio demonstration</h3><button id="tone">Play synthetic 440 Hz tone</button>
<svg class="wv" viewBox="0 0 400 60" preserveAspectRatio="none"><path id="aw"/></svg>
<p class="mu">Synthetic demonstration tone, not a received broadcast.</p><p class="mu det" style="font-size:.9rem">Synthetic demonstration tone, <b>not</b> a received broadcast. Loudness follows the model's recovered-audio level. The audio tone (hundreds of Hz) rides as the <i>envelope</i> on a carrier hundreds of thousands of times faster.</p></div></section>

<section id="learn"><h2>Technical learning</h2><p class="mu">Tap a topic to expand.</p>
<div class="eq">f₀ = 1 / (2π√(LC))</div>
<p>The tuned circuit selects the radio-frequency signal. The nonlinear detector recovers the modulation envelope. The earphone converts the resulting electrical audio into sound. The detector creates no energy and does not generate the broadcast; the received radio signal supplies all energy, so the audio is usually very weak.</p>
<div id="lr"></div></section>

