// ---------- learning ----------
const LR=[["What is a Foxhole Radio?","A very simple passive AM receiver. The name comes from improvised radios built by soldiers from scrap, often with a blade and graphite as a detector. It has an antenna, tuned circuit, detector and earphone, and no power supply."],
["How AM works","The transmitter varies the amplitude of a high-frequency carrier (530–1700 kHz) so its envelope follows the audio: v(t) = A[1 + m·x(t)]·cos(2πf_c t), with modulation index m ≤ 1."],
["What radio waves carry","Electromagnetic waves carry energy and, through modulation of the carrier, information. The wave itself is RF; the audio is only in the slow envelope."],
["Why an antenna receives","The wave's changing electric field drives charges along a conductor, inducing a small RF voltage (microvolts to millivolts). Longer, higher wires usually pick up more."],
["How an LC circuit selects a frequency","Inductor and capacitor exchange energy. At f₀ = 1/(2π√LC) the circuit gives its strongest response; other frequencies are attenuated. Example: L = 250 µH and C = 101 pF give f₀ ≈ 1.0 MHz."],
["What resonance means","A driving signal matching the natural frequency of the circuit builds large oscillation. The sharpness is described by Q, set by losses; low Q means poor station separation."],
["Blade + graphite as crude detector","Some metal–graphite or metal–oxide contacts show asymmetric, nonlinear current–voltage behavior at a suitable point. It is contact-sensitive and not a guaranteed modern diode."],
["Rectification and envelope detection","A nonlinear contact conducts more in one direction (rectification), so the average current follows the envelope. Capacitance in the circuit and the earphone smooth out the RF, leaving audio."],
["Why no powered amplifier is needed","The earphone is driven directly by energy collected from the radio wave. No gain is added: power out is less than power in. The result is quiet and works best near strong stations."],
["Why high impedance earphone","The recovered current is tiny. A high-impedance, sensitive transducer turns small current at higher voltage into sound; low-impedance earbuds draw too much current for the signal."],
["Why reception can be weak or unreliable","Weak stations, poor antenna or return, coil losses, stray capacitance, off-tune settings and a fickle detector contact all reduce output."],
["Limitations","Low volume, poor selectivity, fragile contact, sensitivity to placement, and strong local stations can overlap. This site's simulation is conceptual and does not guarantee reception."]];
$("lr").innerHTML=LR.map((a,i)=>`<details ><summary>${i+1}. ${a[0]}</summary><p>${a[1]}</p></details>`).join("");
